{"subjectId":"python","id":"enums","title":"Enums in Depth","group":"Object-Oriented Python","order":37,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">An <code>Enum</code> is a fixed set of named, unique constants. Members are singletons, are iterable in definition order, and make invalid states unrepresentable — no more magic strings and numbers.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>Basics: names, values, lookup</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from enum import Enum\n\nclass Color(Enum):\n    RED = 1\n    GREEN = 2\n    BLUE = 3\n\nprint(Color.RED, repr(Color.RED))            # Color.RED &lt;Color.RED: 1&gt;\nprint(Color(2), Color[\"BLUE\"])               # Color.GREEN Color.BLUE\nprint(Color.RED.name, Color.RED.value)       # RED 1\nprint(len(Color), [c.name for c in Color])   # 3 ['RED', 'GREEN', 'BLUE']\nprint(Color.RED == 1, Color.RED is Color(1)) # False True\ntry:\n    Color(7)\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e)                                 # 7 is not a valid Color</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>You have</th><th>You want</th><th>Write</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a value (e.g. from a DB)</td><td>the member</td><td><code>Color(2)</code></td></tr><tr><td>a name (e.g. from config)</td><td>the member</td><td><code>Color['GREEN']</code></td></tr><tr><td>a member</td><td>its name / value</td><td><code>m.name</code> / <code>m.value</code></td></tr><tr><td>the class</td><td>all members</td><td><code>list(Color)</code> or <code>Color.__members__</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Members are <strong>singletons</strong>, so compare them with <code>is</code> (or <code>==</code>). A plain <code>Enum</code> member never equals its raw value: <code>Color.RED == 1</code> is <code>False</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-wand\"></i>auto() and generated values</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from enum import Enum, auto\n\nclass Status(Enum):\n    TODO = auto()\n    DOING = auto()\n    DONE = auto()\n\nclass Method(Enum):\n    @staticmethod\n    def _generate_next_value_(name, start, count, last_values):\n        return name.lower()                # customise what auto() produces\n    GET = auto()\n    POST = auto()\n\nprint([s.value for s in Status])           # [1, 2, 3]\nprint(Method.POST.value)                   # post</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>Aliases and @unique</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from enum import Enum, unique\n\nclass Shape(Enum):\n    SQUARE = 2\n    CIRCLE = 3\n    BOX = 2                                  # same value → an alias of SQUARE\n\nprint(Shape.BOX, Shape(2))                   # Shape.SQUARE Shape.SQUARE\nprint([s.name for s in Shape], list(Shape.__members__))   # ['SQUARE', 'CIRCLE'] ['SQUARE', 'CIRCLE', 'BOX']\n\ntry:\n    @unique\n    class Bad(Enum):\n        A = 1\n        B = 1\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e)                                 # duplicate values found in &lt;enum 'Bad'&gt;: B -&gt; A</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-category\"></i>IntEnum, StrEnum &amp; Flag</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from enum import IntEnum, StrEnum, auto\n\nclass Priority(IntEnum):\n    LOW = 1\n    HIGH = 3\n\nclass Env(StrEnum):                          # 3.11+\n    DEV = auto()                             # StrEnum's auto() → the lower-cased name\n    PROD = auto()\n\nprint(Priority.HIGH &gt; Priority.LOW, Priority.HIGH + 1)   # True 4\nprint(sorted([3, Priority.LOW]))                         # [&lt;Priority.LOW: 1&gt;, 3]\nprint(Env.PROD, Env.PROD == \"prod\", Env.DEV.upper())     # prod True DEV\nprint(f\"api-{Env.DEV}.example.com\")                     # api-dev.example.com</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from enum import Flag, auto\n\nclass Perm(Flag):\n    READ = auto()        # 1\n    WRITE = auto()       # 2\n    EXEC = auto()        # 4\n    RW = READ | WRITE    # a named combination\n\np = Perm.READ | Perm.EXEC\nprint(p)                                         # Perm.READ|EXEC\nprint(Perm.READ in p, Perm.WRITE in p)           # True False\nprint(Perm.RW.value, [m.name for m in Perm.RW])  # 3 ['READ', 'WRITE']\nprint(p &amp; ~Perm.EXEC, bool(Perm(0)))             # Perm.READ False</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Members are also…</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Enum</code></td><td>nothing else</td><td>Default choice — strict, can't be mixed up with ints/strings</td></tr><tr><td><code>IntEnum</code></td><td><code>int</code>s</td><td>Interop with code/protocols that expect integers (HTTP status, DB codes)</td></tr><tr><td><code>StrEnum</code></td><td><code>str</code>s</td><td>Values go into JSON, URLs, logs, config files</td></tr><tr><td><code>Flag</code> / <code>IntFlag</code></td><td>bit flags</td><td>Combinable options: permissions, feature toggles</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>IntEnum</code>/<code>StrEnum</code> members compare equal to raw ints/strings — convenient, but it gives up some of the type safety that makes enums useful. Prefer plain <code>Enum</code> unless you need the interop.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Methods, properties &amp; data on members</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from enum import Enum\n\nclass Planet(Enum):\n    MERCURY = (3.303e23, 2.4397e6)\n    EARTH = (5.976e24, 6.37814e6)\n\n    def __init__(self, mass, radius):       # the tuple value is unpacked into __init__\n        self.mass, self.radius = mass, radius\n\n    @property\n    def gravity(self):\n        return round(6.673e-11 * self.mass / self.radius ** 2, 2)\n\n    @classmethod\n    def heaviest(cls):\n        return max(cls, key=lambda p: p.mass)\n\nprint(Planet.EARTH.gravity, Planet.heaviest().name)   # 9.8 EARTH</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary-tree\"></i>Enums in match &amp; JSON</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import json\nfrom enum import Enum, StrEnum\n\nclass Light(Enum):\n    RED = \"stop\"\n    YELLOW = \"slow\"\n    GREEN = \"go\"\n\ndef action(light):\n    match light:\n        case Light.RED | Light.YELLOW:        # dotted names are value patterns\n            return \"brake\"\n        case Light.GREEN:\n            return \"drive\"\n\nprint(action(Light.YELLOW), action(Light(\"go\")))              # brake drive\n\nclass Role(StrEnum):\n    ADMIN = \"admin\"\n\nprint(json.dumps({\"role\": Role.ADMIN}))                       # {\"role\": \"admin\"}\ntry:\n    json.dumps({\"light\": Light.RED})\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"plain Enum is not JSON serializable\")              # plain Enum is not JSON serializable\nprint(json.dumps({\"light\": Light.RED}, default=lambda e: e.value))   # {\"light\": \"stop\"}\nprint(Light(json.loads('{\"light\": \"stop\"}')[\"light\"]))       # Light.RED</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview angle</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why use an Enum instead of string or int constants?</summary><div><p>Typos become <code>AttributeError</code>s instead of silent bugs, the set of valid values is explicit and iterable, members carry a readable name in logs and reprs, and type checkers can verify that a function only receives valid members (and that a <code>match</code> handles all of them).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you convert between an enum and its stored value?</summary><div><p><code>Member.value</code> to store it (DB/JSON), <code>EnumClass(value)</code> to get the member back (raises <code>ValueError</code> for unknown values), and <code>EnumClass[name]</code> to look up by name.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is an enum alias?</summary><div><p>A second name defined with the same value. It resolves to the first member (<code>Shape.BOX is Shape.SQUARE</code>) and is skipped during iteration. Decorate the class with <code>@unique</code> to forbid aliases.</p></div></details></section>"}